  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Areas of fog or low clouds again possible tonight.\n\n-  Light precipitation is possible Thursday onward.\n\nNear-Term (Today):\nStrong temperature inversions and moisture from regional \nwaterways (like the 50-degree Flathead Lake) have locked in \nfog and low clouds across northwest to west-central Montana \nthis morning. Expect these clouds to largely erode by this \nafternoon. However, clouds may linger in the Mission and Flathead \nValleys and around Flathead Lake, which would keep high \ntemperatures cooler than forecast. Caution: Freezing fog may \ncreate icy, slick spots on bridges and overpasses this morning.\n\nTonight - Thursday: Another round of fog or low clouds is\npossible. A weak shortwave trough will traverse the area on \nThursday. For the valleys, it will mainly bring \ncloudy skies with low clouds possibly lingering in a few narrow\nvalleys of northwest Montana. Snow showers above 5000 feet are\npossible in the mountains with minimal accumulations. \n\nFriday \u2013 Saturday Morning: Leftover Pacific moisture will keep \nskies mostly cloudy, with lingering mountain showers focused over \nnorthwest Montana.\n\nSaturday Afternoon: A break between systems will allow for a mild \nday with above-average temperatures and increasing high clouds.\n\nSunday: A flattening ridge brings breezy southwest winds and a \nrenewed chance of showers from north-central Idaho to northwest \nMontana.\n\nEarly next week: High pressure keeps temperatures above average \nand most areas dry, though light precipitation remains possible \nfrom central Idaho to NW Montana. Nightly valley fog remains a \nthreat. Longer-range models hint at a much more unsettled, wet \npattern arriving by mid-month.",
